{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q002_mazg_hise_remote_homology","answer":"Verified SCOPe facts (release 2.08, stable, released 2021-09-22): d1vmga_ (1vmg A:, sunid 113676) is classified a.204.1.2 in family MazG-like (sunid 116993), protein 'Hypothetical protein SSo12199 (SSo3215)', species Sulfolobus solfataricus. d1y6xa1 (1y6x A:7-93, sunid 122676) is classified a.204.1.4 in family HisE-like (PRA-PH) (sunid 140797), protein 'Phosphoribosyl-ATP pyrophosphatase HisE', species Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Both share class a (All alpha proteins, sunid 46456), fold a.204 (all-alpha NTP pyrophosphatases, sunid 101385), and superfamily a.204.1 (all-alpha NTP pyrophosphatases, sunid 101386). The lowest shared node is the superfamily. Annotation and homology sections for both domains contain no warnings, heterogeneity flags, repeats, or inconsistencies; d1vmga_ carries only the comments 'Structural genomics target' and 'complexed with li, unl'.

Relationship SCOPe supports: same superfamily, different families. This is SCOPe's positive assertion of probable common ancestry (remote homology) inferred from structural, functional, and sequence evidence, while the separate family placement indicates the sequence relationship is not close enough for family-level membership. It is stronger than the shared-fold-only similarity that would apply for different superfamilies, and weaker than the close relationship a shared family would imply.

Conclusions that would go beyond the evidence: (1) Specific catalytic function does not transfer. Superfamily membership does not establish that the Sulfolobus protein hydrolyzes phosphoribosyl-ATP, nor that HisE acts on MazG-type NTP/dNTP substrates; substrate, reaction, ligand, and pathway role require active-site, ligand, or experimental evidence outside SCOPe. (2) The Sulfolobus domain's function is unverified: its SCOPe protein node is a hypothetical protein and its ligand is annotated as unknown, so calling it a confirmed MazG enzyme or pyrophosphatase from the family name alone overstates the data. (3) Oligomeric state, domain architecture, and genomic context do not transfer; d1y6xa1 covers only residues 7-93 of its chain and SCOPe says nothing about the rest of either protein. (4) Neither structure is validated as a modeling template for the other: no structural alignment, RMSD, residue mapping, or topology comparison was retrieved, so template suitability is not established. (5) No representative-quality ranking: SPACI/AEROSPACI records for 1vmg and 1y6x were not retrieved. (6) No historical claim about when or why the families were grouped, since only release 2.08 was queried.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__scope__compare_scop_domains","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1vmga_","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1vmga_/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1vmga_/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1vmga_/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1y6xa1","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1y6xa1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1y6xa1/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1y6xa1/homology","scop-homology/SKILL.md","scop-homology/references/biological-interpretation.md"]}]}
